  <section class="hub-section hub-section-gray">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2>How our techs approach outdoor patio heaters</h2>
      <p>Patio-heater failures fall into two main categories by architecture: gas units (Bromic, Sunpak, Schwank) — most failures trace to thermocouple or pilot ignition, gas-valve service, or wind-related flame instability. Electric infrared units (Infratech, Solaira, AEI/Indeeco) — most failures trace to element burnout (often after summer dormancy + first cool-night activation), controller issues, or wiring degradation in coastal salt air. Infratech specifically (manufactured in Burbank) has the strongest parts availability locally — we keep Comfort/Motif/CD element replacements on the truck for the common sizes.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="hub-section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why does my patio heater stop working in the spring after winter idle?</summary>
          <p>Element burnout on electric infrareds — common after summer dormancy. The element cools, accumulates moisture, then arcs on first re-energization. Replace the affected element ($245-$385 typically). On gas units, the analog issue is thermocouple oxidation during idle months.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Is Infratech really better than the imports?</summary>
          <p>For LA specifically, yes. Manufactured in Burbank, so parts are available next-day or same-day on common items. Element quality is consistently good. The Comfort, Motif, and C/CD series cover most luxury patio installations we service in Beverly Hills, Malibu, and Pacific Palisades.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Do you work on hotel rooftop patio heaters?</summary>
          <p>Yes. Rooftop installations are routine — we coordinate with hotel facilities management for safe access. Bromic gas units are common on hotel rooftops; we're set up for high-altitude gas service and the wind-shielding considerations specific to rooftop installs.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

